description:
I'd love generalizable versions of `rstudioapi::isAvailable()` that can detect in which environment code is running to then gate logic (e.g., authentication patterns that differ b/w Workbench and local), to prevent ad hoc picking of env vars (that may drift over product versions).

A starting point translated from some wrappers I've used:
```
# duplicative of `rstudioapi::IsAvailable()` but avoids circular logic if `rstudioapi` is not installed
get_env_rstudio_ide <- function() {
  Sys.getenv("RSTUDIO_PROGRAM_MODE")
}

is_env_rstudio_ide_desktop <- function() {
  get_env_rstudio_ide() == "desktop"
}

is_env_rstudio_ide_workbench <- function() {
  get_env_rstudio_ide() == "server"
}

is_env_rstudio_ide <- function() {
  is_env_rstudio_ide_desktop() || is_env_rstudio_ide_workbench()
}

is_env_connect <- function() {
  # have also used `USER` or `USERNAME`
  Sys.getenv("LOGNAME") == "rstudio-connect"
}

# Processes executing on a server (Workbench or Connect) ----
is_env_server <- function() {
  is_env_rstudio_ide_workbench() || is_env_connect()
}
```

Related to https://github.com/r-lib/httr2/pull/410/files

As a package author I want to my objects to render well in all environments.  Thanks to recent work on knitr (see https://github.com/rstudio/rmarkdown/issues/649 ), we will have the `knitr::is_html_output()` and `knitr::is_latex_output()` functions to determine the output format we (package authors) should target.  However, it seems difficult to me ([and to others](
https://stackoverflow.com/questions/41197626/determine-if-script-is-running-within-an-rstudio-notebook)) to determine when our code is being run from a R notebook environment, and we need to distinguish that from being run from the console to present the right embedded output.

See this notebook as a simple example (requires `devtools::install_github("yihui/knitr")` for the `is_*_output` functions)

    ---
    title: "R Notebook"
    output: html_notebook
    ---
    
    ```{r}
    # Define a custom S3 class object
    new_myclass <- function() {
      return(structure(list(a = 1, b = 2), class = "myclass"))
    }

    # Functions that return HTML, LaTeX and plain text given a class
    myclass2html <- function(obj) { return("<p>MyClass object as <em>HTML</em></p>")}
    myclass2latex <- function(obj) { return("\\begin{em}MyClass\\end{em} object $x$")}
    myclass2char <- function(obj) { return("MyClass object")}
    
    # Function to print in the right format:
    knit_print.myclass <- function(x, ...) {
      if (knitr::is_html_output()) {
        htmltools::HTML(myclass2html(x))
      } else if (knitr::is_latex_output()) {
        knitr::asis_output(myclass2latex(x))
      } else {
        warning("This format is not that well supported") # could be .docx or other
        knitr::knit_print(myclass2char(x))
      }
    }
    # print object:
    knitr::knit_print(new_myclass())
    
    ```

When I use the "run chunk" button or when I use the preview option I get the plain text output. However I would rather get the HTML output embedded below the code. Is there a way for RStudio to report to knitr that the html output should be preferred in these cases?

The following gadget pastes an assignment twice. This happens with any code I put inside

```
library(miniUI)
myApp <- function(){
  ui <- miniPage(
    gadgetTitleBar("Test"),
    miniContentPanel(
        selectInput("value","Select a value",
                    choices = letters[1:3])
    )
  )
  
  server <- function(input, output, session){
    observeEvent(input$done, {
      text <- paste0("x <- ", input$value)
      rstudioapi::sendToConsole(text)
      stopApp()
    })
  }
  
  viewer <- dialogViewer("a text",300, 200)
  runGadget(ui, server, viewer = viewer)
}

myApp()
```
